<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Amin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">787–813</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad al-Amin (caliph)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Amin was the sixth Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, who reigned from 809 to 813. His attempt to exclude his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, governor of Khurasan, from the succession led to a civil war, the Fourth Fitna. Al-Ma'mun's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahir-ibn-husayn">Tahir ibn Husayn</a> defeated al-Amin's armies and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and al-Amin was killed after the city fell in 813.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-amin.jpg" alt="al-Amin" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DrFO.Jr.Tn · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbasid_Dinar_-_Al_Amin_-_195_AH_(811_AD).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
